    {
      "paperId": "/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-98315-3_13",
      "doi": "10.1007/978-3-031-98315-3_13",
      "title": "Astronomy from Space",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Renato Dicati"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2026,
      "abstract": "With the advent of space telescopes, the entire electromagnetic spectrum has become accessible to astronomers. The development of rockets and artificial satellites, between the 1940s and 1950s, allowed astronomers and scientists to carry observation instruments above the atmosphere to also detect IR, UV, X-ray, and gamma radiation. In a short time, new astronomical disciplines have been developed that complement each other and which, due to the complexity of the instrumentation used, the resources employed and the results obtained, now dominate traditional astronomy. Many celestial sources discovered by optical astronomers and radio astronomers can now be studied in other wavelengths and new objects discovered in other bands (e.g., gamma-ray bursts) can now be monitored by means of radio observations and those conducted from the ground with large optical telescopes of new technology. Radio astronomy is no longer a separate and distinct field, but a facet of multi-wavelength astronomy although it retains unique astronomical and technical characteristics.",
      "abstract_not_available": false,
      "snippet": "With the advent of space telescopes, the entire electromagnetic spectrum has become accessible to astronomers. The development of rockets and...",
